---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import { cardText, release } from '../lib/data';
import { usDate } from '../lib/dates';

const contact = cardText('contact_address');
---

<Base
  title="About"
  section="About"
  description="Why the Common English OpenList exists, who makes it, its sister projects, its licenses, how language models enter its data, and how to reach it."
>
  <div class="frame about">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The Common English OpenList is made by Ryan Kamp. It grew out of the English OpenList, a word list with its own
        rules, which the CEOL Universe now holds as one of its parts.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="about-grid">
      <section aria-labelledby="why">
        <h2 id="why">Why it exists</h2>
        <p>
          Ask whether a string is a word, and the answer depends on which dictionary you ask. Dictionaries and word lists
          disagree, for several reasons:
        </p>
        <ul class="reasons">
          <li><strong>Timing:</strong> They are updated at different times, and some are no longer updated at all.</li>
          <li><strong>Scope:</strong> Specialized and technical terms are in scope for some and not for others.</li>
          <li><strong>Standards:</strong> Some leave out slang or profanity on purpose.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Definitions:</strong> Each has its own rules for what a word is, such as whether it may hold a space, a
            hyphen, or an accent, and its own way of taking in a new entry, from a community of editors or from an
            institution's staff.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The CEOL Universe does not settle the question. For each candidate, it records which published lists hold it,
          which could not have held it, and how it came in, in versions fixed at a date, so that whoever settles the
          question can see the evidence.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="family">
        <h2 id="family">The family</h2>
        <dl class="pairs">
          <div>
            <dt><a href="/">The CEOL Universe</a></dt>
            <dd>The inventory: every candidate, with its provenance, in versions that never change once released.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><a href="/eol/">The English OpenList</a></dt>
            <dd>A word list with its own rules, kept here as a dated subset of the universe.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><a href="https://ars-magna.pages.dev/" rel="noopener">Ars Magna</a></dt>
            <dd>An anagram finder that shares the English OpenList's dictionary.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="licenses">
        <h2 id="licenses">Licenses</h2>
        <dl class="pairs">
          <div>
            <dt>The data</dt>
            <dd>CC BY-SA 4.0, as the terms of Wiktionary and of wordfreq require. Every version ships its sources'
              notices.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>The code</dt>
            <dd>MIT.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>This site's text</dt>
            <dd>CC BY-SA 4.0, except <a href="/research/report/">the technical report</a>, which is CC BY 4.0.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="more"><a href="/dataset/sources/">Sources, licenses, and credits</a></p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="models">
        <h2 id="models">Language models in the data</h2>
        <p>
          The English OpenList decided some of the entries of its valid list with a language model, Google Gemini 3 Flash
          Preview: through a verification pipeline that used it, or by its verdict alone. Its synthetic engine, whose code
          does not survive, made other strings by affixing, by chemical substitution, by scientific prefixing, and by Greek
          plurals; <a href="/eol/#how">the English OpenList's page</a> gives its author's account of the methods. No row
          says which entries a model decided: that is a fact of how the English OpenList took a word in, and its own
          records no longer say it either. The universe marks the synthetic engine's rows in <code>model_or_engine</code>,
          so a list can keep or drop them.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/compose/?d.model_or_engine=none">Compose a list without the engine's rows</a></p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="contact">
        <h2 id="contact">Requests and contact</h2>
        <p>
          Requests to withhold, correct, or restore a source or a row go to
          <a class="email" href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>. A request is acknowledged within 7 days and acted on
          within 14 days of its verification, and each version's change log records every withholding, as counts.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/dataset/cite/">How to cite it</a></p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="version">
        <h2 id="version">This version</h2>
        <p>
          Version {release.name}, built {usDate(release.builtOn)}: <Figure id="rows" /> rows. A released version never changes; a
          correction comes as a new version with a change log.
        </p>
        <p class="more"><a href="/dataset/versions/">Versions and changes</a></p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="author">
        <h2 id="author">The author</h2>
        <p>Ryan Kamp makes the Common English OpenList and its sister projects.</p>
        <ul class="links">
          <li><a href="https://ryanjosephkamp.github.io/" rel="noopener">ryanjosephkamp.github.io</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/ryanjosephkamp" rel="noopener">github.com/ryanjosephkamp</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .email {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .about {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-7);
  }
  .page-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-5);
  }
  .about-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 24rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-7) var(--space-8);
  }
  .about-grid section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    align-content: start;
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    max-width: 40rem;
  }
  .about-grid h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
  }
  .about-grid p {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .pairs {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
  .pairs dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .pairs dt a {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .pairs dt a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
  .pairs dd {
    margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .more {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .reasons {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .links {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-1);
  }
</style>
